WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.392
[MUSIC]

00:00:02.392 --> 00:00:06.552
In our last episode, we discussed
the psychology of grit and how leaders and

00:00:06.552 --> 00:00:09.153
enterprises can be built
to withstand crisis.

00:00:09.153 --> 00:00:12.003
So we thought it was the perfect
time to revisit an interview

00:00:12.003 --> 00:00:14.210
from one of our very first episodes.

00:00:14.210 --> 00:00:15.369
In this masterclass,

00:00:15.369 --> 00:00:19.334
we'll hear from Stanford Graduate School
of Business Professor Baba Shiv,

00:00:19.334 --> 00:00:22.750
whose research focuses on
the neuroscience of decision making and

00:00:22.750 --> 00:00:26.482
the practice of innovation,
from Silicon Valley to emerging markets.

00:00:26.482 --> 00:00:30.403
&gt;&gt; One thing you gotta recognize
is that at the end of the day,

00:00:30.403 --> 00:00:35.302
most human decisions and most human
behaviors are grounded in emotion.

00:00:35.302 --> 00:00:37.246
It is not the rational brain.

00:00:37.246 --> 00:00:40.664
&gt;&gt; In his experience surveying
hundreds of CEOs over the years,

00:00:40.664 --> 00:00:44.292
Professor Shiv has seen companies
deal with all sorts of crises.

00:00:44.292 --> 00:00:47.499
Some have flourished,
but some have folded.

00:00:47.499 --> 00:00:51.611
In this deep dive, we'll discuss
what distinguishes the survivors,

00:00:51.611 --> 00:00:56.356
the mental and physical ways that leaders
can prepare to innovate during crises.

00:00:56.356 --> 00:01:00.711
And some distinct advantages shared by
entrepreneurs in emerging markets when

00:01:00.711 --> 00:01:01.561
a crisis hits.

00:01:01.561 --> 00:01:07.572
[MUSIC]

00:01:07.572 --> 00:01:10.998
I'm Darius Teter, and this is
a masterclass by Grit &amp; Growth with

00:01:10.998 --> 00:01:14.363
Stanford Graduate School of Business,
the show where Africa and

00:01:14.363 --> 00:01:18.176
South Asia's intrepid entrepreneurs
share their trials and triumphs.

00:01:18.176 --> 00:01:21.721
[MUSIC]

00:01:21.721 --> 00:01:24.994
Much of Professor Shiv's work
is focused on the difference

00:01:24.994 --> 00:01:28.144
between what he calls the type one and
type two mindsets.

00:01:28.144 --> 00:01:29.982
I'll let him explain.

00:01:29.982 --> 00:01:34.200
&gt;&gt; If you have read Daniel Kahneman's book
Thinking, Fast and Slow, it's exactly what

00:01:34.200 --> 00:01:37.665
he says, that most of our decisions
are made by fast thinking systems,

00:01:37.665 --> 00:01:41.457
what I call emotional systems,
not by slow thinking deliberative systems.

00:01:41.457 --> 00:01:44.902
We're going to understand how
does the emotional brain work.

00:01:44.902 --> 00:01:48.982
The way the emotional brain is geared to
behave at any given point in time will

00:01:48.982 --> 00:01:51.151
depend upon the mindset that prevails or

00:01:51.151 --> 00:01:54.042
the mental state that prevails
at that point in time.

00:01:54.042 --> 00:01:58.501
Is it a type one mindset or
is it a type two mindset?

00:01:58.501 --> 00:02:00.908
So these are terms that
are borrowed from statistics.

00:02:00.908 --> 00:02:04.510
In statistics, we have the notion of
the type one error, the type two error.

00:02:04.510 --> 00:02:05.806
What is the type one error in statistics?

00:02:05.806 --> 00:02:08.891
The fear of making a mistake,
it's a fear of failure.

00:02:08.891 --> 00:02:12.331
The type two mindset, on the other hand,
is not a fear of failure,

00:02:12.331 --> 00:02:14.735
it's a fear of missing
out on opportunities.

00:02:14.735 --> 00:02:18.107
At the end of the day,
it's really not a fear, it is a desire for

00:02:18.107 --> 00:02:19.265
new opportunities.

00:02:19.265 --> 00:02:22.870
And it comes out that the brain has
two distinct neural circuitries,

00:02:22.870 --> 00:02:25.419
one devoted to the risk
averse type one mindset,

00:02:25.419 --> 00:02:28.359
another devoted to the risk
tolerant type two mindset.

00:02:28.359 --> 00:02:33.224
And the most critical thing about the way
the emotional brain is geared to behave at

00:02:33.224 --> 00:02:37.455
any given point in time is that if
the brain is still in a state of stress,

00:02:37.455 --> 00:02:40.158
in other words,
still in a type one mindset.

00:02:40.158 --> 00:02:44.364
The brain will be unable to switch to
the risk tolerant type two mindset.

00:02:44.364 --> 00:02:49.036
And that's why it's so crucial that
you first get into a state of comfort.

00:02:49.036 --> 00:02:54.528
This could be, for individuals, could be
in the form of getting good night's sleep,

00:02:54.528 --> 00:02:56.951
fitness also makes a big difference.

00:02:56.951 --> 00:03:00.951
Practices like meditation, etc., makes a
big difference because it's going to make

00:03:00.951 --> 00:03:02.842
the brain a lot more resilient to stress,

00:03:02.842 --> 00:03:05.182
which means that the leader
is not often stressed.

00:03:05.182 --> 00:03:09.272
Which means that leader's brain can switch
into the risk tolerant type two mindset.

00:03:09.272 --> 00:03:11.959
And there's a lot of
research backing this up,

00:03:11.959 --> 00:03:16.393
that something like 90 to 95% of human
decisions and behaviors are shaped

00:03:16.393 --> 00:03:20.983
non-consciously by emotional brain
systems, not by rational brain systems.

00:03:20.983 --> 00:03:25.641
&gt;&gt; So in Baba Shiv's lexicon, the type
one mindset worries about failure,

00:03:25.641 --> 00:03:29.035
while the type two mindset
is open to opportunities.

00:03:29.035 --> 00:03:33.250
I wondered how these mindsets
actually affect decision making.

00:03:33.250 --> 00:03:36.974
&gt;&gt; At the end of the day, what a rational
brain is good at is simply rationalizing

00:03:36.974 --> 00:03:39.515
what the emotional brain
has already decided to do.

00:03:39.515 --> 00:03:43.145
Which means if you're stuck in a risk
averse mindset, the rational brain will

00:03:43.145 --> 00:03:46.335
come in and say, these are the reasons
why you have to be risk averse,

00:03:46.335 --> 00:03:48.981
these are the reasons why you
should not innovate, etc.

00:03:48.981 --> 00:03:53.334
Whereas if you're in type two mindset,
the rational brain will come in and say,

00:03:53.334 --> 00:03:56.293
here are the reasons why you
should take some chances.

00:03:56.293 --> 00:04:00.392
And then you can start thinking about
the downstream applications of the simple

00:04:00.392 --> 00:04:03.817
principle of the emotional brain
having the type one circuitry and

00:04:03.817 --> 00:04:05.122
the type two circuitry.

00:04:05.122 --> 00:04:09.989
You'll see some leaders, when the crisis
hits, they will have done all the scenario

00:04:09.989 --> 00:04:13.520
planning beforehand,
they might have plan B, plan C, etc.

00:04:13.520 --> 00:04:17.747
But that is really very cognitive
in nature, that exercise.

00:04:17.747 --> 00:04:22.170
It is when they get into the crisis
when the true leadership capabilities

00:04:22.170 --> 00:04:23.559
will begin to emerge.

00:04:23.559 --> 00:04:27.417
And you can see that happening among
leaders that when the crisis hits,

00:04:27.417 --> 00:04:29.679
they're able to kind of take a step back.

00:04:29.679 --> 00:04:32.629
They're able to assess the situation and

00:04:32.629 --> 00:04:37.777
then think very creatively about what
can I do to address the challenge.

00:04:37.777 --> 00:04:39.465
But even more important,

00:04:39.465 --> 00:04:43.927
how can I leverage the opportunities
that this crisis has afforded?

00:04:43.927 --> 00:04:49.192
[MUSIC]

00:04:49.192 --> 00:04:51.695
&gt;&gt; Of course,
the mind is part of the body, and

00:04:51.695 --> 00:04:54.683
our mindset is affected by
our physical condition.

00:04:54.683 --> 00:04:57.932
So what can we do to
achieve a type two mindset?

00:04:57.932 --> 00:05:00.211
Here's what Baba Shiv recommends.

00:05:00.211 --> 00:05:03.732
&gt;&gt; How should a leader go
about managing a crisis?

00:05:03.732 --> 00:05:06.766
First and foremost,
the person needs to come down.

00:05:06.766 --> 00:05:11.400
First thing I would advise
the leaders to do is get good sleep.

00:05:11.400 --> 00:05:16.654
What tends to happen during crises is
that leaders are fighting a lot of fires,

00:05:16.654 --> 00:05:19.372
and what they would sacrifice is sleep.

00:05:19.372 --> 00:05:22.459
And lack of sleep is a huge detriment.

00:05:22.459 --> 00:05:25.775
Because what'll happen is that
if you're not had enough sleep,

00:05:25.775 --> 00:05:29.936
what is called restorative sleep, when
all the brain chemicals like serotonin,

00:05:29.936 --> 00:05:32.365
dopamine, etc., are coming back to normal.

00:05:32.365 --> 00:05:35.933
You're going to naturally, from
a physiological and a brain standpoint,

00:05:35.933 --> 00:05:39.461
you're naturally going to wake up being
in a risk averse type one mindset.

00:05:39.461 --> 00:05:44.069
That's the last thing that the leader
wants to do in times of crisis.

00:05:44.069 --> 00:05:46.031
So get good night's rest.

00:05:46.031 --> 00:05:49.332
If you can let your team do that,
do that as well.

00:05:49.332 --> 00:05:54.661
Because what is needed out here is not
just the leader, but also the entire team

00:05:54.661 --> 00:06:00.184
that is on board to kind of wade through
the crisis, addressing the challenges.

00:06:00.184 --> 00:06:01.385
But more importantly,

00:06:01.385 --> 00:06:04.754
grabbing the opportunities that
the crisis is going to be afforded.

00:06:04.754 --> 00:06:08.384
[MUSIC]

00:06:08.384 --> 00:06:13.163
The second thing that I would do is that,
even before any crisis hits,

00:06:13.163 --> 00:06:19.090
a leader should get into practices
like meditation, yoga, tai chi, etc.

00:06:19.090 --> 00:06:23.008
Now, this might sound like some kind
of yoga master kind of teaching, no.

00:06:23.008 --> 00:06:27.911
There's a lot of science behind this
suggesting that if you engage in practices

00:06:27.911 --> 00:06:32.666
like tai chi, yoga, meditation,
etc., that slows down your breathing,

00:06:32.666 --> 00:06:36.421
your brain is going to become
a lot more resilient to stress.

00:06:36.421 --> 00:06:39.363
So when a crisis hits,
get good night's sleep.

00:06:39.363 --> 00:06:43.998
And if you're not yet practicing, the
leader is not yet practicing meditation,

00:06:43.998 --> 00:06:48.856
tai chi, yoga etc., slowing down their
breath, they should start that practice.

00:06:48.856 --> 00:06:53.128
Just to make sure that the brain is
able to go into the innovative or

00:06:53.128 --> 00:06:55.543
the type two risk tolerant mindset.

00:06:55.543 --> 00:06:59.418
&gt;&gt; There are even ways to physically
measure how prepared your mind is

00:06:59.418 --> 00:07:00.671
to deal with stress.

00:07:00.671 --> 00:07:04.953
&gt;&gt; So technology is going to definitely
help us going forward, right?

00:07:04.953 --> 00:07:09.684
[COUGH] So if you think about how
resilient your brain is to stress is

00:07:09.684 --> 00:07:14.862
highly correlated with what is
referred to as heart rate variability.

00:07:14.862 --> 00:07:18.676
Heart rate variability essentially taps
into the parasympathetic nervous system.

00:07:18.676 --> 00:07:21.494
So we have the sympathetic nervous
system and the parasympathetic.

00:07:21.494 --> 00:07:24.792
The sympathetic is
the fight-flight system and

00:07:24.792 --> 00:07:30.270
the parasympathetic is the rest-digest
system, calm down, calm down, etc.

00:07:30.270 --> 00:07:34.092
If you want to know if your brain
is inherently resilient to stress,

00:07:34.092 --> 00:07:37.122
you can easily monitor your
heart rate variability.

00:07:37.122 --> 00:07:40.060
There are a lot of ways of doing that,
you can do this with your phone, there

00:07:40.060 --> 00:07:43.252
are other ones that are out there, you
can measure your heart rate variability.

00:07:43.252 --> 00:07:46.446
And the heart rate variability
essentially is the following.

00:07:46.446 --> 00:07:48.072
The heart doesn't beat like a metronome.

00:07:48.072 --> 00:07:51.634
We think that it is beating at
regular intervals, it is not.

00:07:51.634 --> 00:07:54.513
There's always a fluctuation
between beats, so

00:07:54.513 --> 00:07:56.850
it is called beat-to-beat interval.

00:07:56.850 --> 00:07:59.870
The beat-to-beat interval will
always have a fluctuation.

00:07:59.870 --> 00:08:02.700
The greater the fluctuation,
the greater the variability.

00:08:03.760 --> 00:08:06.233
In other words,
greater the heart rate variability,

00:08:06.233 --> 00:08:08.180
the more resilient the brain is to stress.

00:08:09.620 --> 00:08:13.721
So one of the things that a leader
could do, thanks to technology is to

00:08:13.721 --> 00:08:17.485
monitor the heart rate variability
at any given point in time.

00:08:17.485 --> 00:08:21.342
Which will tell them, are they in
a position right now to make a decision,

00:08:21.342 --> 00:08:22.662
an important decision?

00:08:22.662 --> 00:08:26.767
[MUSIC]

00:08:26.767 --> 00:08:30.918
&gt;&gt; Many leaders in times of crisis
fall back on tried and true products,

00:08:30.918 --> 00:08:32.680
customers and markets.

00:08:32.680 --> 00:08:36.710
They go with what they know, or
they take a wait and see approach.

00:08:36.710 --> 00:08:39.900
That may seem reasonable, but is it right?

00:08:39.900 --> 00:08:43.170
&gt;&gt; So let's think about it,
why is crisis the best time to innovate?

00:08:44.230 --> 00:08:48.207
It is Charles Darwin who said that it is
not the strongest of the species that

00:08:48.207 --> 00:08:51.110
survives, not even the most intelligence.

00:08:51.110 --> 00:08:56.419
The one that is going to survive is
the one that is most adaptable to change.

00:08:56.419 --> 00:09:01.283
Which means that yes, you can say,
as a leader, I'll just wait this out, for

00:09:01.283 --> 00:09:04.620
six months, fine,
I'm going to emerge from there.

00:09:04.620 --> 00:09:06.735
Maybe I'll emerge weaker, but

00:09:06.735 --> 00:09:11.480
I'll kind of start from that
point in time and move forward.

00:09:11.480 --> 00:09:13.730
But keep in mind,
you're not alone in the market.

00:09:13.730 --> 00:09:15.100
There are other players in the market.

00:09:15.100 --> 00:09:18.957
And if there's another player in
the market, who, as Darwin said,

00:09:18.957 --> 00:09:21.099
is much more adaptable to the change.

00:09:21.099 --> 00:09:25.243
They are going to emerge much stronger
than you are going into the future, so

00:09:25.243 --> 00:09:28.483
don't assume that your strength
before the crisis is going

00:09:28.483 --> 00:09:30.391
to remain the same going forward.

00:09:30.391 --> 00:09:33.443
Because there are other players out there
who are probably been doing the right kind

00:09:33.443 --> 00:09:36.407
of things in terms of being innovative,
adaptive etc, will go and emerge much,

00:09:36.407 --> 00:09:37.900
much stronger.

00:09:37.900 --> 00:09:42.140
So the survival of the company
in the long haul, never,

00:09:42.140 --> 00:09:46.120
ever comes from sticking
to the status quo.

00:09:46.120 --> 00:09:49.138
It always will come from
being adaptable to change,

00:09:49.138 --> 00:09:51.930
responding to change that is happening.

00:09:51.930 --> 00:09:55.595
And that is where the leader needs
to have an innovation mindset and

00:09:55.595 --> 00:09:59.339
the organization as a culture needs
to have an innovation mindset.

00:10:00.450 --> 00:10:05.200
What they would do is that, they always
treat the situation as being dynamic.

00:10:05.200 --> 00:10:06.210
It is not static.

00:10:06.210 --> 00:10:11.286
What it means is that, you have come into
the situation with a lot of assumptions,

00:10:11.286 --> 00:10:13.710
preconceived notions, etc.

00:10:13.710 --> 00:10:16.543
That's the same thing for leaders as well,

00:10:16.543 --> 00:10:20.142
a crisis is a great opportunity for
leaders to come in and

00:10:20.142 --> 00:10:24.980
re-assess all the assumptions and
preconceived notions that they have.

00:10:26.230 --> 00:10:31.194
Don't try to do what you were doing in the
past because that is not going to help you

00:10:31.194 --> 00:10:32.520
address the crisis.

00:10:32.520 --> 00:10:35.730
Because you cannot assume that
the world is static anymore.

00:10:35.730 --> 00:10:38.023
A crisis is going to be very dynamic.

00:10:38.023 --> 00:10:39.649
[MUSIC].

00:10:39.649 --> 00:10:41.454
&gt;&gt; Baba Shiv has a simple shorthand for

00:10:41.454 --> 00:10:44.910
leaders who bring an innovation
mindset to a crisis.

00:10:44.910 --> 00:10:50.260
Innovation equals creativity times
execution and divided by constraints.

00:10:50.260 --> 00:10:54.610
And no, that's not a mathematical formula,
but for Baba, it does add up.

00:10:54.610 --> 00:10:55.740
&gt;&gt; Generally if I,

00:10:55.740 --> 00:11:01.329
if you should ask people of what comes to
mind when the word innovation is uttered.

00:11:01.329 --> 00:11:04.790
In general,
people will say innovation is creativity.

00:11:06.210 --> 00:11:08.280
Innovation is really not just creativity.

00:11:08.280 --> 00:11:10.312
Creativity is an important
component of that,

00:11:10.312 --> 00:11:13.860
that requires the innovation mindset,
the type two mindset etc.

00:11:13.860 --> 00:11:16.480
But it also requires execution.

00:11:16.480 --> 00:11:20.540
You are coming up with certain
hypotheses that need to be tested, and

00:11:20.540 --> 00:11:22.970
that requires execution.

00:11:22.970 --> 00:11:24.940
The third component here is constraints.

00:11:24.940 --> 00:11:28.728
So the way I look at it this, is that
innovation is creativity multiplied

00:11:28.728 --> 00:11:33.350
by execution, divided by perceived
constraints or the constraints out there.

00:11:33.350 --> 00:11:35.862
The second way to think about
constraints is that, and

00:11:35.862 --> 00:11:38.054
I've seen this happening time and
time again.

00:11:38.054 --> 00:11:42.964
If I tell you to come up with a solution,
so for example, you're going to Rwanda,

00:11:42.964 --> 00:11:45.527
you're looking at a rural home out there,

00:11:45.527 --> 00:11:49.350
the integrity of the flooring and
the home is not great.

00:11:49.350 --> 00:11:52.694
And if I'm going to tell you, you need
to come up with a solution for this.

00:11:52.694 --> 00:11:56.052
Your first thing that you will do is
to say, okay, I'll just make sure,

00:11:56.052 --> 00:11:59.356
I'll just find out how much it costs
to put cement and so on and so forth,

00:11:59.356 --> 00:12:04.100
not realizing that person will not be able
to spend more than $50 for the floor.

00:12:04.100 --> 00:12:05.450
That is a constraint.

00:12:05.450 --> 00:12:09.924
So if you bring in constraints very
early on in the design process as you're

00:12:09.924 --> 00:12:13.200
figuring out, what is working,
what is not working?

00:12:13.200 --> 00:12:14.430
What are the assumptions to be made?

00:12:14.430 --> 00:12:16.340
What are the hypotheses etc.

00:12:16.340 --> 00:12:19.810
It is always a good idea to
bring in the constraints.

00:12:19.810 --> 00:12:23.445
So two things quickly to summarize
about this equation that innovation is

00:12:23.445 --> 00:12:25.550
creativity multiplied by execution.

00:12:25.550 --> 00:12:27.780
Yes, creativity is important.

00:12:27.780 --> 00:12:29.620
Yes, execution is important.

00:12:29.620 --> 00:12:32.210
But let's not forget the constraints.

00:12:32.210 --> 00:12:34.355
And especially in a crisis situation,

00:12:34.355 --> 00:12:38.610
you're bound to face constraints
that are never faced before.

00:12:38.610 --> 00:12:40.320
It could be financial constraints.

00:12:40.320 --> 00:12:42.290
It could be market constraints.

00:12:42.290 --> 00:12:43.740
It could be team constraints.

00:12:43.740 --> 00:12:47.516
Maybe a team member has fallen sick,
a critical team member has fallen sick,

00:12:47.516 --> 00:12:49.520
those are all constraints.

00:12:49.520 --> 00:12:54.404
And innovation comes from being
able to develop your solution and

00:12:54.404 --> 00:12:59.440
deliver on your solution
subject to these constraints.

00:12:59.440 --> 00:13:02.990
Always a good idea to incorporate
constraints into the design process.

00:13:04.010 --> 00:13:07.181
&gt;&gt; Baba Shiv has seen firsthand
that leaders from emerging markets

00:13:07.181 --> 00:13:09.340
are particularly resourceful.

00:13:09.340 --> 00:13:12.490
Not in spite of their situations,
but because of them.

00:13:12.490 --> 00:13:16.223
What I noticed about entrepreneurs
who come out from India, Africa,

00:13:16.223 --> 00:13:19.580
be it East Africa,
West Africa, Southern Africa.

00:13:19.580 --> 00:13:22.838
One thing I noticed is that
they are extremely innovative,

00:13:22.838 --> 00:13:27.590
extremely innovative because they
are facing constraints all the time.

00:13:27.590 --> 00:13:31.690
That could be credit constraints,
interest rates are very high.

00:13:31.690 --> 00:13:34.198
There is a lack of trust
between the supplier and

00:13:34.198 --> 00:13:36.660
they're dealing with all the time.

00:13:36.660 --> 00:13:40.014
And I feel that rather than saying
that that is a disadvantage of

00:13:40.014 --> 00:13:44.790
being an entrepreneur down the economy,
I would argue it is actually an advantage.

00:13:44.790 --> 00:13:49.037
Because inherently you're a lot more
innovative in terms of the creativity,

00:13:49.037 --> 00:13:53.270
in terms of execution and going with
the constraints that are out there.

00:13:53.270 --> 00:13:56.939
You're far better equipped to handle
crisis than someone who is probably in

00:13:56.939 --> 00:14:00.200
the more Western,
more developed kind of economies.

00:14:00.200 --> 00:14:04.040
And also one of the things that they
quickly realize in these developing

00:14:04.040 --> 00:14:06.920
economies is that,
because of all the constraints,

00:14:06.920 --> 00:14:10.540
because of all the struggles
they have to go through.

00:14:10.540 --> 00:14:14.734
When given an opportunity to collaborate
with other business leaders,

00:14:14.734 --> 00:14:18.379
they jump onto that opportunity
much sooner than people in more

00:14:18.379 --> 00:14:20.177
developed economies will do.

00:14:20.177 --> 00:14:22.612
Because in developed
economies you can say, yeah,

00:14:22.612 --> 00:14:25.225
I'm going to raise more money and
I can solve the problem.

00:14:25.225 --> 00:14:27.315
Yeah, I can hire more people,
I can solve the problem.

00:14:27.315 --> 00:14:31.395
Yeah, I can pay more or pay less and
give a discount I can solve the problem.

00:14:31.395 --> 00:14:35.012
The business leaders in many of
the developing economies that I have

00:14:35.012 --> 00:14:38.513
encountered out there,
don't go with that kind of assumption.

00:14:38.513 --> 00:14:40.843
The first thing is not
going to raise more money,

00:14:40.843 --> 00:14:45.090
is it who can I reach out to in my
network so that I can get some help?

00:14:45.090 --> 00:14:49.474
And that, it's a very natural process that
they go through which is why you're seeing

00:14:49.474 --> 00:14:53.230
now, we are seeing this right in
front of our eyes as we speak.

00:14:53.230 --> 00:14:57.098
All the collaboration that is happening
among the business leaders who have

00:14:57.098 --> 00:14:58.375
attended the C program.

00:14:58.375 --> 00:15:01.571
[MUSIC]

00:15:01.571 --> 00:15:03.991
&gt;&gt; Part of what makes a crisis
such a fertile time for

00:15:03.991 --> 00:15:07.240
innovation is that it turns
the world on its head.

00:15:07.240 --> 00:15:10.526
&gt;&gt; If you go with that assumption
that the world is dynamic,

00:15:10.526 --> 00:15:14.437
what that allows you to do is first
get rid of preconceived notions.

00:15:14.437 --> 00:15:19.316
And start looking at what new assumptions
I have to make going forward, and

00:15:19.316 --> 00:15:22.450
how do I go about testing
those assumptions?

00:15:22.450 --> 00:15:27.320
It's one thing to simply go about coming
up with new assumptions out there.

00:15:27.320 --> 00:15:29.051
The other thing is to
actually execute on that,

00:15:29.051 --> 00:15:31.300
which means you need to
test these things out.

00:15:31.300 --> 00:15:36.484
So I would always advocate a leader
during a crisis to consider the situation

00:15:36.484 --> 00:15:40.946
to be dynamic, even more so
when we are not in a crisis situation.

00:15:40.946 --> 00:15:45.083
Go back to the drawing board and
ask the fundamental questions,

00:15:45.083 --> 00:15:49.073
is that what assumptions have I
come into the situation with.

00:15:49.073 --> 00:15:53.432
That is all assumptions that are come from
the past, what assumptions need to be

00:15:53.432 --> 00:15:57.261
relaxed, what new assumptions or
hypothesis need to be formed.

00:15:57.261 --> 00:15:59.774
Ask yourself what is
the business that you're in.

00:15:59.774 --> 00:16:04.105
And very often I see entrepreneurs
making the mistake they say that,

00:16:04.105 --> 00:16:07.250
I'm in the business of
selling pineapple juice.

00:16:07.250 --> 00:16:10.514
No, you're not in the business
of selling pineapple juice,

00:16:10.514 --> 00:16:14.846
you're in the business of quenching thirst
with a beverage that tastes good, and

00:16:14.846 --> 00:16:18.020
is also healthy, and is also nutritious.

00:16:18.020 --> 00:16:22.561
Okay, so if that is how you define
your business, why rely on pineapple,

00:16:22.561 --> 00:16:27.326
I might find a local fruit in the country
where I can utilize my same production

00:16:27.326 --> 00:16:32.118
facility, to create a beverage that is
no longer dependent upon pineapple.

00:16:32.118 --> 00:16:35.604
If it works, then how do you replicate
that, if it doesn't work that's fine.

00:16:35.604 --> 00:16:39.106
You at least learned, you'll fail fast,
you'll fail cheap, and

00:16:39.106 --> 00:16:43.468
you'll fail forward, in the sense that
you'll learn something from the situation

00:16:43.468 --> 00:16:46.682
that you can now apply to
the crisis situation going forward.

00:16:46.682 --> 00:16:50.063
[MUSIC]

00:16:50.063 --> 00:16:53.809
It is in terms of crisis that
the true mettle of a leader,

00:16:53.809 --> 00:16:56.190
is going to be determined.

00:16:56.190 --> 00:17:01.023
And which is why, if you go to a VC and
you are a startup, you've started failed,

00:17:01.023 --> 00:17:05.143
you go to for the next venture,
you go and knock on the door of a VC, and

00:17:05.143 --> 00:17:06.734
say I want to raise money.

00:17:06.734 --> 00:17:10.977
The VC is not going to say the last
business you started and you failed.

00:17:10.977 --> 00:17:15.669
What they're going to say is that, you
will learn a lot from the failure that you

00:17:15.669 --> 00:17:19.540
went through, which means you're
going to be a lot more mature.

00:17:19.540 --> 00:17:23.679
Which means you're not going to repeat
the same mistakes that you committed in

00:17:23.679 --> 00:17:24.981
the previous startup.

00:17:24.981 --> 00:17:27.690
&gt;&gt; Part of innovation is messing up.

00:17:27.690 --> 00:17:32.187
But in many cultures, the fear of
failure can stifle experimentation.

00:17:32.187 --> 00:17:35.638
&gt;&gt; We 2007/2008 time period,
the Department of Science and

00:17:35.638 --> 00:17:38.543
Technology in India had asked
me to come down and do some,

00:17:38.543 --> 00:17:41.892
work with them about how do we
foster entrepreneurship in India.

00:17:41.892 --> 00:17:45.720
I started talking to some of the best
students from the best universities,

00:17:45.720 --> 00:17:47.981
most of them engineering schools in India.

00:17:47.981 --> 00:17:51.802
And I was shocked to learn that the top
10% of the class, irrespective of

00:17:51.802 --> 00:17:55.442
the university I went to and
asked the students, you want to innovate,

00:17:55.442 --> 00:17:58.860
you want to become an entrepreneur,
but let me ask you a question?

00:17:58.860 --> 00:18:01.910
You can either be an innovator or
you can be an entrepreneur, and

00:18:01.910 --> 00:18:03.675
that's a big difference out there.

00:18:03.675 --> 00:18:06.697
You can innovate stuff and
then sell off the IP to someone else.

00:18:06.697 --> 00:18:08.855
Entrepreneur requires you
actually have to run a company.

00:18:08.855 --> 00:18:11.937
And I asked every one of these
students that have met up,

00:18:11.937 --> 00:18:14.046
would you like to be an entrepreneur?

00:18:14.046 --> 00:18:17.661
And invariably,
this is 2007 time period, they said no.

00:18:17.661 --> 00:18:19.339
And I asked, why?

00:18:19.339 --> 00:18:21.345
And that's how you got to understand
the culture of the place.

00:18:21.345 --> 00:18:25.057
Imagine that a young kid
in an engineering school,

00:18:25.057 --> 00:18:30.470
okay, 20, 22 years old,
has a job offer from the Tatas.

00:18:30.470 --> 00:18:35.614
The person cannot make career decisions on
their own, that's the culture of India,

00:18:35.614 --> 00:18:40.051
and many of the Asian countries, and
also countries in Africa by the way.

00:18:40.051 --> 00:18:44.191
Where, if I have to make these career
decisions I have to consult my parents.

00:18:44.191 --> 00:18:46.467
I have to consult my uncle,
my aunt ,and so on and so forth,

00:18:46.467 --> 00:18:47.934
there are multiple people out there.

00:18:47.934 --> 00:18:51.223
If I'm this young person going
to my parents and saying,

00:18:51.223 --> 00:18:55.470
I have a job offer from the Tatas, but
I want to go into entrepreneurship,

00:18:55.470 --> 00:18:58.025
what do you think the parents
are going to say?

00:18:58.025 --> 00:19:01.363
[LAUGH] They're going to say, [LAUGH]
you are stupid, is how I brought you up?

00:19:01.363 --> 00:19:05.824
And therefore, when you're designing the
solutions you gotta be acutely aware of

00:19:05.824 --> 00:19:08.449
the cultural context
that we're operating in.

00:19:08.449 --> 00:19:12.364
And it's absolutely right that
in most cultures out there,

00:19:12.364 --> 00:19:17.984
failure is not tolerated, failure is
ridiculed, sometimes even punished, right?

00:19:17.984 --> 00:19:19.766
That's kind of state out there.

00:19:19.766 --> 00:19:22.593
Hopefully what the Graduate School
of Business is doing,

00:19:22.593 --> 00:19:25.708
because that is the mission of
the Graduate School of Business,

00:19:25.708 --> 00:19:28.838
is to change lives,
change organizations, change the world.

00:19:28.838 --> 00:19:31.880
Is for us to create this ecosystems,

00:19:31.880 --> 00:19:36.110
where failure is no longer
going to be seen as double.

00:19:36.110 --> 00:19:40.760
Yes it is going to take time, but the hope
is that within one generation 20 years

00:19:40.760 --> 00:19:45.480
that we think about, these delicate
economies where this culture of failure or

00:19:45.480 --> 00:19:49.510
against failure is so deeply embedded,
we're looking to change.

00:19:49.510 --> 00:19:54.278
And we're beginning to see the change,
I mean from 2007 to 2017,

00:19:54.278 --> 00:19:58.908
there was a massive change in what I
was observing on the ground in India.

00:19:58.908 --> 00:20:03.561
Where now young kids in the top 10% of
their class, they don't want to go and

00:20:03.561 --> 00:20:08.091
work for a large corporation, which is
no different than the Stanford GSB.

00:20:08.091 --> 00:20:12.479
They want to work in a startup, either an
early state or growth state startup, why?

00:20:12.479 --> 00:20:14.830
Because that taboo has disappeared.

00:20:14.830 --> 00:20:16.000
And why does that happen?

00:20:16.000 --> 00:20:18.630
That's because now there are role models.

00:20:18.630 --> 00:20:21.498
There are people who started
companies like Flipkart in India,

00:20:21.498 --> 00:20:23.640
etc, who become very successful.

00:20:23.640 --> 00:20:27.421
Now the parents are saying, yeah you have
an offer from the Tatas but I don't want

00:20:27.421 --> 00:20:30.831
you to go and work in the Tatas out there,
that is a previous generation.

00:20:30.831 --> 00:20:33.773
I would rather you go and
start a company and

00:20:33.773 --> 00:20:36.809
make it into the next
Flipkart of the world.

00:20:36.809 --> 00:20:39.642
[MUSIC]

00:20:39.642 --> 00:20:41.864
&gt;&gt; Baba has outlined a series of steps for

00:20:41.864 --> 00:20:44.997
a leader to get in the right
mindset to tackle crises.

00:20:44.997 --> 00:20:49.394
From challenging assumptions while
recognizing constraints, to searching for

00:20:49.394 --> 00:20:52.335
opportunities to innovate,
and embracing failure.

00:20:52.335 --> 00:20:56.353
But I wondered,
how does a leader bring their team along,

00:20:56.353 --> 00:21:01.037
how do they foster a type two
mindset throughout the organization?

00:21:01.037 --> 00:21:02.634
&gt;&gt; If everyone is stressed,

00:21:02.634 --> 00:21:06.926
generally what tends to happen is
people will go into their own shell.

00:21:06.926 --> 00:21:11.117
Because it's a survival mode, I have
to survive first before I start helping

00:21:11.117 --> 00:21:13.644
others and
start collaborating with others.

00:21:13.644 --> 00:21:18.414
It is the leader's job to kind of say,
let us go through this whole thing.

00:21:18.414 --> 00:21:23.463
Forget about whether you win or
lose, ask yourself the question,

00:21:23.463 --> 00:21:27.806
are you going to feel proud
with yourself of what you did?

00:21:27.806 --> 00:21:30.540
And you going to feel proud,
when you're giving your best.

00:21:31.570 --> 00:21:32.942
And that's the leader's job.

00:21:32.942 --> 00:21:37.861
The leader's job is to have the internal
team members, get out of the state of

00:21:37.861 --> 00:21:42.188
stress, knowing that our survival
is going to come from teamwork.

00:21:42.188 --> 00:21:46.619
And the second critical factor
that they have to be looking at,

00:21:46.619 --> 00:21:49.127
the leaders have to be doing is, for

00:21:49.127 --> 00:21:54.414
the team members to kind of put in place
the culture and the space out there.

00:21:54.414 --> 00:21:59.283
So that they can start now reaching out
and collaborating among one another.

00:21:59.283 --> 00:22:00.346
And that's a leader's job.

00:22:00.346 --> 00:22:04.512
The leader's job is to tell the staff
out there that, it is not about losing,

00:22:04.512 --> 00:22:08.130
it's not about winning out there,
but let us feel proud.

00:22:08.130 --> 00:22:12.176
And let us be proud in such a way
that when we emerge from the crisis,

00:22:12.176 --> 00:22:14.870
we will have emerged much stronger.

00:22:14.870 --> 00:22:17.284
Not the company, not the leader, but

00:22:17.284 --> 00:22:21.600
all of us collectively have emerged
from it feeling much stronger.

00:22:21.600 --> 00:22:24.061
Failures could have happened, that's okay.

00:22:24.061 --> 00:22:28.705
[MUSIC]

00:22:28.705 --> 00:22:33.336
&gt;&gt; One thing we've learned over the past
18 months, is that big exogenous shocks

00:22:33.336 --> 00:22:37.489
like the pandemic are experienced not
just as a crisis for each company,

00:22:37.489 --> 00:22:39.600
but as a series of interrelated, and

00:22:39.600 --> 00:22:43.649
sometimes reinforcing crises
throughout supply chains in markets.

00:22:44.700 --> 00:22:49.200
So focusing all your energy internally,
maybe a missed opportunity.

00:22:49.200 --> 00:22:53.440
I asked Baba how leaders can draw
on external resources in crisis.

00:22:53.440 --> 00:22:57.140
Do leaders have potential
allies with shared interests?

00:22:57.140 --> 00:23:00.679
&gt;&gt; People often talk about competitive
advantage, and don't get me wrong,

00:23:00.679 --> 00:23:02.420
that is important, in other words,

00:23:02.420 --> 00:23:06.510
how is your value proposition superior
than that of your nearest competitor?

00:23:06.510 --> 00:23:08.281
That's a competitive advantage.

00:23:08.281 --> 00:23:12.005
But people forget that there is a second
element which is referred to as

00:23:12.005 --> 00:23:13.530
collaborative advantage.

00:23:13.530 --> 00:23:16.183
Now, collaborative advantage, very often,

00:23:16.183 --> 00:23:19.490
is about how do you
manage your dependencies?

00:23:19.490 --> 00:23:22.265
So you might have suppliers out there,
you have customers out there,

00:23:22.265 --> 00:23:24.960
your have distributors out there,
these are all relationships.

00:23:24.960 --> 00:23:29.090
What do we often end up doing in trying
to create a collaborative advantage?

00:23:29.090 --> 00:23:31.580
We adopt a transactional approach.

00:23:31.580 --> 00:23:34.174
The transaction approach means that
we're going to sign some contracts.

00:23:34.174 --> 00:23:38.013
If there's any violation of the contracts,
then we can go to the court of law and

00:23:38.013 --> 00:23:40.582
have that adjudicated,
and so on, and so forth.

00:23:40.582 --> 00:23:41.405
But guess what?

00:23:41.405 --> 00:23:46.200
In a transactional approach,
will give rise to a lot of suspicion.

00:23:46.200 --> 00:23:47.919
People are suspecting one another,

00:23:47.919 --> 00:23:50.477
they don't divulging
information with one another.

00:23:50.477 --> 00:23:53.615
It's an adversarial
kind of a relationship.

00:23:53.615 --> 00:23:57.599
The true collaborate advantage comes
when one adopts a relational approach,

00:23:57.599 --> 00:24:00.027
not a transactional but
relational approach.

00:24:00.027 --> 00:24:04.333
And if you go to many of the Asian
countries, you'll kind of notice that.

00:24:04.333 --> 00:24:09.144
Go to Japan, for example, you will never
be able to break into the Japanese market

00:24:09.144 --> 00:24:13.749
unless you have created a very strong,
deep relationship with the key partner

00:24:13.749 --> 00:24:18.657
that you're looking for in Japan, because
everything is based on relationship.

00:24:18.657 --> 00:24:22.850
And at the core of relationship,
the relationship approach of creating

00:24:22.850 --> 00:24:25.681
collaborative advantage,
is building trust.

00:24:25.681 --> 00:24:30.916
[MUSIC]

00:24:30.916 --> 00:24:34.698
It is in times of crisis when everyone
is struggling, it's not just you,

00:24:34.698 --> 00:24:39.092
you're not alone in this, there are other
people also who are struggling out there.

00:24:39.092 --> 00:24:43.199
How do we go to these important
strategic partners, and suppliers, and

00:24:43.199 --> 00:24:46.579
distributors, etc,
not with a transactional approach?

00:24:46.579 --> 00:24:51.184
And guess what, when you adopt that kind
of relational approach that your partner

00:24:51.184 --> 00:24:54.467
has also been doing,
people will feel grateful for that.

00:24:54.467 --> 00:24:58.326
They will always remember you
that in times of difficulty,

00:24:58.326 --> 00:25:02.955
when even your partner didn't have
the money to pay salaries, etc.,

00:25:02.955 --> 00:25:06.600
that leader was able to do
something to help people out.

00:25:06.600 --> 00:25:09.236
And people never forget that,
never, ever forget that.

00:25:09.236 --> 00:25:12.032
And you're going to develop
a deep level relationship,

00:25:12.032 --> 00:25:13.849
not only amongst your team members.

00:25:13.849 --> 00:25:16.045
And your partner is doing the right thing,

00:25:16.045 --> 00:25:19.656
they're developing that kind of
camaraderie to monitor the prices.

00:25:19.656 --> 00:25:22.899
But even your strategic partners
out there, your suppliers and

00:25:22.899 --> 00:25:26.273
distributors, are going to kind
of look at you very differently.

00:25:26.273 --> 00:25:29.851
That you're not just here for
a transactional gain,

00:25:29.851 --> 00:25:34.622
where it's just an exchange that is
taking place, but you're here for

00:25:34.622 --> 00:25:38.137
a collaborative gain that
all of us are going to win.

00:25:38.137 --> 00:25:40.404
[MUSIC]

00:25:40.404 --> 00:25:44.487
&gt;&gt; I asked Baba Shiv to summarize
some actionable takeaways for

00:25:44.487 --> 00:25:46.016
CEOs facing a crisis.

00:25:46.016 --> 00:25:48.258
&gt;&gt; So let me give some thoughts here for

00:25:48.258 --> 00:25:51.335
any leader who is going through a crisis,
okay.

00:25:51.335 --> 00:25:57.426
First one, remember that you are in
a unique position to not only survive,

00:25:57.426 --> 00:26:01.018
but emerge from the crisis being stronger.

00:26:01.018 --> 00:26:02.803
And you have the capabilities to do that.

00:26:02.803 --> 00:26:05.661
And if you don't, you gotta build it.

00:26:05.661 --> 00:26:10.055
The second one that I would say is,
always think about not just competitive

00:26:10.055 --> 00:26:14.420
advantage, think also about
collaborative advantage.

00:26:14.420 --> 00:26:18.574
And think about collaborative advantage
that comes not from just a transactional

00:26:18.574 --> 00:26:20.990
approach, comes from
a relational approach.

00:26:22.160 --> 00:26:27.510
And what it means, is building meaningful
connections that are grounded in trust.

00:26:29.130 --> 00:26:32.919
If you're already in a crisis right now,
hopefully you have prepared for

00:26:32.919 --> 00:26:34.020
the crisis.

00:26:34.020 --> 00:26:37.409
So keep in mind that the people who
are better prepared are going to be

00:26:37.409 --> 00:26:38.920
the winners.

00:26:38.920 --> 00:26:41.270
But you can't prepare for
every single crisis.

00:26:41.270 --> 00:26:44.180
So preparation is not just
about scenario planning.

00:26:44.180 --> 00:26:47.180
Preparation is not just about thinking
about various strategies, and

00:26:47.180 --> 00:26:48.740
outcomes, and so on.

00:26:48.740 --> 00:26:53.606
Preparation comes from the fact
that do I have the capabilities,

00:26:53.606 --> 00:26:59.369
which is the emotional capabilities,
to deal with the crisis, number one.

00:26:59.369 --> 00:27:03.041
And number two,
does my team have the capabilities,

00:27:03.041 --> 00:27:07.700
the emotional capabilities,
to be innovative at times of crisis.

00:27:09.220 --> 00:27:13.364
And the third takeaway is that it
is a leader's job to make sure that

00:27:13.364 --> 00:27:16.038
the organization is adaptable to change.

00:27:16.038 --> 00:27:20.568
And it's a leader's job to ensure
that that organization emerges from

00:27:20.568 --> 00:27:23.068
the crisis a lot stronger than before.

00:27:23.068 --> 00:27:25.931
And at the root of that
is an innovation mindset.

00:27:25.931 --> 00:27:28.801
[MUSIC]

00:27:28.801 --> 00:27:33.598
&gt;&gt; Baba Shiv believes companies that take
these steps are better positioned to win

00:27:33.598 --> 00:27:34.535
in the future.

00:27:34.535 --> 00:27:39.268
&gt;&gt; If you think about the long
term success of any organization,

00:27:39.268 --> 00:27:45.001
in my humble opinion, that is going to
come from times of crisis like this,

00:27:45.001 --> 00:27:46.833
and this is universal.

00:27:46.833 --> 00:27:51.608
But what these leaders would kind of learn
from this is the importance of maintaining

00:27:51.608 --> 00:27:55.928
connections with one another,
meaningful connections with one another.

00:27:55.928 --> 00:28:00.608
And creating a collaborative advantage
that is truly grounded in meaningful

00:28:00.608 --> 00:28:05.436
connections, truly grounded in trust,
rather than a transaction approach.

00:28:05.436 --> 00:28:08.491
So I believe that this
period that we are in,

00:28:08.491 --> 00:28:13.448
where the leaders from Africa,
